{"connection_id": "BC-915644364632d3588f7a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.130.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Waiting more than watchmen for morning", "note": "The commentary turns patience into a posture held across night toward morning; the psalmist repeats that his soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen wait for morning."}

{"connection_id": "BC-cb65dbd64e62ae1d1659", "ref": "WLC:Ps.136.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sun governs the day", "note": "The commentary's two daily periods are each assigned a place; the litany names the sun as ruler of the day, with its companion night assignment in the next verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4d3390d8aca4d87326c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.136.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moon and stars govern the night", "note": "The commentary pairs night with day as adjacent ordered parts; the litany gives the night to moon and stars after naming the sun's daily rule in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3acee0d2c73af197b34", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Days written before any existed", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes a long time before human existence with life's later stages; the psalm says the speaker's days were recorded when none of them had yet come, placing the life course in divine foreknowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-528b6f0eafdf4822832f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.144.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Days as a passing shadow", "note": "The commentary compresses lifetime into a brief seeming stay; the psalm says human days resemble a shadow that passes away."}
{"connection_id": "BC-478319fb6ba1fc7c4aaa", "ref": "WLC:Ps.148.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ḥ-q-q / Hebrew חקק: enduring cosmic decree", "note": "The table's sound correspondence is unverified; God establishes the heavenly bodies permanently and gives them a decree that will not pass, an ordinance behind the commentary's dependable recurring periods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a515ecb4761607294ffe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.19.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Day speaks to day, night to night", "note": "The commentary pictures adjoining periods as a chain; the psalm has each day pour speech into the next and each night disclose knowledge to the next, making succession itself communicative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-228cf08a00912ecd2ba7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.25.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Truth taught while waiting all day", "note": "The commentary places counsel in truth and patience inside the day's span; the psalmist asks to be taught God's truth and says he waits for God all day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb14780a568deb7154c6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.30.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "night weeping; morning joy", "note": "The commentary presents night and day as attached parts; this verse makes the transition morally meaningful, with weeping lodged in the night and joy arriving in the morning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-62a9b40c1936aecf57ef", "ref": "WLC:Ps.31.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "My times are in your hand", "note": "The commentary places the pieces and limits of a person's life under God's ordering; the psalmist entrusts his own times to God's hand while asking deliverance from enemies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a18caefa395b4c6a7434", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Be still and wait despite evildoer success", "note": "The commentary says patience must be sustained while truth is not yet manifest; the psalm tells its hearer to wait patiently for God rather than fret over a person succeeding in an evil course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f952e741ed66ff74706", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth piled up beside shadow-like life", "note": "The commentary says the race to accumulate numbers consumes time until the graves; this verse places צבר, piling up wealth, beside human life passing like a shadow and not knowing who will gather the pile. The table's ص ب ر / צבר sound correspondence is unverified, and Hebrew means heap rather than the commentary's patience, so the link is the surrounding accumulation-and-mortality motif, not a shared sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b90ba05cceafa7de0b7e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Accumulation and ignorance of its heir", "note": "The commentary recalls the preceding surah's race to multiply possessions until death; the psalm says a person heaps wealth without knowing who will gather it after him. Hebrew צבר is the table's sound correspondence to Arabic ṣ-b-r, but this row rests on the developed accumulation motif, not a claimed shared meaning with patience."}

{"connection_id": "BC-4cfb04122bfb8be6e569", "ref": "WLC:Ps.42.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daytime care and nighttime song", "note": "The commentary makes night and day adjoining parts of a lived religious interval; the psalmist expects God's kindness by day and a song-prayer with him at night."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55289d1833f583080900", "ref": "WLC:Ps.49.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Accumulated wealth cannot go through death", "note": "The commentary recalls multiplying possessions until the grave; the psalm says a wealthy person carries none of it away when dying, specifying the limit that accumulation cannot cross."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a581b8ce82f3db19a62", "ref": "WLC:Ps.55.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "evening, morning, and noon prayer", "note": "The commentary names night/day and morning/evening pairs and describes patience across the day; this psalm's speaker prays and is heard at evening, morning, and noon, extending the paired hours into a recurring daily practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-615946c5a7f89c400e16", "ref": "WLC:Ps.55.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Evening, morning, noon prayer", "note": "The commentary explicitly pairs morning with late day; the psalmist names evening, morning, and noon as repeated times of appeal to God, dividing the day into acts of persistence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb7d1e9338c2e5ea0882", "ref": "WLC:Ps.71.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Old age and the next generation", "note": "The commentary joins successive life stages to people sustaining one another; the aged speaker asks not to be forsaken until he tells the next generation of God's power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-beb2e9a3f6850fec9fcf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.74.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Day and night belong to God", "note": "The commentary treats the paired periods as more than an undifferentiated flow; the psalm addresses God as owner of both day and night and maker of the luminaries that mark them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90b0673c742162b32a6a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.74.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "day and night belong to God; lights established", "note": "The commentary presents day and night as a joined order; this verse says both belong to God and that God established the lights, grounding the temporal pair in divine ordering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff7affe0026f1541afe7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.74.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Summer and winter formed by God", "note": "The commentary moves from daily pairs to winter's middle and cold; this verse attributes the formation of summer and winter, as well as earth's bounds, to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07d1d5e02db6bd35949e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.74.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth's boundaries; summer and winter", "note": "The commentary names seasons and treats time as a sequence of joined portions; the next verse says God fixed the earth's boundaries and made summer and winter, connecting cosmic order to seasonal division."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a4088aed67f6bb5d363", "ref": "WLC:Ps.81.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Horn blown at recurring lunar feast", "note": "The commentary extends morning-evening segments to prescribed seasonal observance; the psalm orders the horn at new moon and full moon for the festival day, whose status as statute is given in the next verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b47ac39531c14ec5cd09", "ref": "WLC:Ps.81.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ḥ-q-q / Hebrew חקק: festival statute", "note": "The root table supplies an unverified sound correspondence; the festival marked in the preceding verse is called a חק for Israel, connecting a recurring appointed day to a shared obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ce922398172df792462", "ref": "WLC:Ps.84.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Good not withheld from upright walkers", "note": "The commentary makes righteous conduct the condition of continuing good through time; the psalm says God withholds no good from those who walk uprightly. The verb uses Hebrew עצר, the package's sound correspondence to Arabic ʿ-ṣ-r, but restraint here has no established cognate link to the commentary's time sense."}

{"connection_id": "BC-6ad8371cca818adb2b74", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Seventy or eighty years pass quickly", "note": "The commentary catalogs life stages but says all that time can contract at the end; the psalm gives a human span of seventy or eighty years and says it quickly passes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1648c8ac8d1f30f82c43", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seventy or eighty years; toil; swift passing", "note": "The commentary follows human life through its stages and says its accumulated years look like only an hour at judgment; this verse measures the ordinary lifespan in decades, then says it passes quickly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5ab5e16ac151f4bd114", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Number days to gain wisdom", "note": "The commentary makes the countable stages of a life matter for righteous action; the psalm asks God to teach humans to count their days so that they acquire a wise heart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d9daeaf352e07264fb9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "numbering one's days", "note": "The commentary says life's duration contracts and that the righteous should remain through it; this prayer asks to be taught to number days so the heart may gain wisdom, turning limited time into ethical instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ae76a22ec3a9c4383de", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A thousand years like yesterday", "note": "The commentary says a long lifetime shrinks to an evening or a daytime hour when viewed at the end; the psalm compares a thousand years before God to yesterday once passed and to a night watch."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4826640d10960b91522", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a thousand years; a watch in the night", "note": "The commentary contrasts long duration with a short sleep interval and a brief end-of-life span; this verse compares a thousand years to yesterday and to a night watch, explicitly collapsing time scales."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0c381ff9919d2f01ab9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Human years swept away like sleep", "note": "The commentary names a short sleep and says a long human span is later perceived as brief; the psalm says God sweeps humans away like sleep before its morning-grass comparison."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca61f7754d647e15ffb0", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Morning growth withers by evening", "note": "The commentary compresses a life into the morning-to-evening interval; the psalm's grass flourishes in the morning and fades by evening, completing the time image begun in the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d613ecabf19a2341da0d", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "morning growth; evening withering", "note": "The commentary names morning/evening as a time pair and says life's long span shrinks to a short part of a day; the psalm's grass sprouts in the morning and withers by evening."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57f79cf59d4393eb4026", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "morning praise; faithfulness by night", "note": "The commentary names dawn/evening and night/day as linked pairs and develops haqq as truth; this liturgical verse assigns lovingkindness to morning praise and faithfulness to night praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81294032fc8b676c3725", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Morning mercy and nightly faithfulness", "note": "The commentary pairs morning and evening as recurring occasions for patient devotion; the sabbath psalm assigns proclamation of God's kindness to morning and his faithfulness to nights."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92dc5dd576ce16eee2a2", "ref": "WLC:Song.2.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Winter has passed", "note": "The commentary calls winter a particular hard interval and ties waiting to its end; the lover announces that winter and its rain are over, so the harsh season has a bounded duration."}

{"connection_id": "BC-c707ba545463dc3686db", "ref": "WLC:Zech.7.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "true judgment and compassion toward one another", "note": "The commentary's final verse has people exhort one another in haqq; Zechariah commands true judgment and compassion each toward a fellow, joining right judgment to reciprocal ethical treatment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b8a24ed259d78491efb", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "speak truth to one another; judgments in the gates", "note": "The commentary's tawasaw bil-haqq is mutual counsel in truth; Zechariah explicitly commands people to speak truth to one another and render true, peaceable judgments, adding a public civic setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58fa1c477734c11844e4", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Speak truth to one's neighbor", "note": "The commentary's mutual counsel is specifically counsel in truth; Zechariah commands each person to speak truth to a neighbor and to render truthful, peace-making judgments."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a74c464ce88bceb83740", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.21", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One group urges another to seek God", "note": "The commentary's people attach themselves to one another through counsel; the city's inhabitants invite others to go entreat God and add that they themselves will go."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15f8bc65754789dc9e0d", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People hold onto another to go together", "note": "The commentary says people are added to one another through a shared orientation to God; Zechariah imagines people of many languages grasping a Jew's garment and asking to go with him because God is with the group."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98b5280a0658426f9f42", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Tyre piles silver before its fall", "note": "The commentary recalls abundance amassed before a terminal reversal; Tyre heaps silver like dust in this verse before its wealth is stripped in the next. Hebrew צבר is the table's sound correspondence to Arabic ṣ-b-r, with no asserted patience cognacy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70e365d1bc5e4717ac93", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "short and sorrowful life; no return from Hades", "note": "The commentary quotes deniers who say time alone destroys them; Wisdom gives the ungodly a speech that life is short and death final, then its larger argument contests their conclusion, adding a Jewish wisdom debate over the denial."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8fa757c5623be33bd366", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The impious call life short and death final", "note": "The commentary reports those who think time alone destroys them; Wisdom assigns to its impious speakers the claim that life is short and no remedy follows death, but the book frames their reasoning as unsound."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d69a1d5ada47e474c87", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "The impious claim death returns them to nonexistence", "note": "The commentary recalls the claim that time alone destroys and counters it with final reckoning; Wisdom puts in the impious speakers' mouths the claim that chance brought them into being and afterward they will be as though they had never existed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a852a88db929d8ec9881", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life passes like a cloud; name and works forgotten", "note": "The commentary says time consumes people and their lifespan contracts; the ungodly in Wisdom say their name and works will be forgotten and life passes like a cloud, dramatizing the same fear of erasure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8d9ad070160b28d0c47", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Life as a passing shadow", "note": "The commentary says the long stay contracts to an evening or an hour; the impious speakers in Wisdom compare life to a passing shadow, though the book presents their conclusion about death as mistaken."}

